iden administration is promising to get  to the bottom of what exactly happened  in china  that led to the start of this pandemic  we need to do that  precisely so we fully understand what  happened in order  to have the best shot possible  preventing it from happening again  lincoln  blaming china for a lack of transparency  when the outbreak began  which he says allowed the virus to  quickly spread throughout the world  jennifer johnson global news washington  still ahead a quebec man's warning about  the virus  after he nearly succumbed to it  a quebec man and his family endured a  terrifying experience  he was so sick with kova 19 that he  spent  months in hospital he almost didn't make  it now he's hoping his story can serve  as a warning amanda delawicki reports  well today's the reflex we'll hopefully  win a prize on a sunny and hot spring  morning peter scufaris enjoys the  company of his wife and daughter  he savors spending time with them  knowing just a few months ago  his life was in danger his body racked  by covid19  it's like a roulette in the casino you  could get  a very simple dose or you can get what i  got  and i never wish it on anybody last  christmas  65 year old peter contracted covid his  three children weren't worried  their dad was healthy and athletic but  within a few days  peter deteriorated struggling to breathe  it was like like lightning speed  the change admitted to the icu doctors  placed him on a ventilator and in a coma  while his body broke down it just  happened so quickly and it was  i think like the worst day of our life  he developed blood clots  double pneumonia and myriad other health  problems as he lay unconscious for  five weeks fighting for his life it  didn't look very good it was in  very bad conditions it was devastating  it was very difficult  it was quite unbelievable we didn't  really couldn't really grasp what was  happening  peter's family never gave up hope he  would recover  and slowly he did  nurses helped him walk again after  losing 40 pounds  in all peter spent almost three months  in hospital  doctors never able to explain why kovitt  attacked him so savagely  he has great lungs how is this happening  and they just said it was it was a big  viral load  when peter finally left the hospital  relieved icu staff clapped as he left  he now faces a long road to recovery  a true family man he believes the  support of his loved ones  helped him survive  i don't think  i could have went through it without  them  yes peter says he has much to look  forward to  i want to hug my kids and i want to hug  my grandkids  that's the first thing and you know of  course i would like to play a round of  golf  a remarkable tale of survival and a  reminder  just how dangerous covert can be amanda  delawaki  global news montreal  up next the path to the cosmos how one  canadian choreographed ballet  and science together  the volcano on the caribbean island of  saint vincent has erupted  again emergency officials can only  describe it as a battle zone  at one point the power was cut and water  is hard to come by  la sifliere first erupted on friday many  have been sleeping in emergency shelters  since residents are told to stay indoors  because of poor air quality  ash has completely covered the  surrounding villages  even barbados which is nearly 200  kilometers away is getting a whiff of it  scientists believe there will be more  eruptions for days or  even weeks when it comes to reaching for  the stars countries like the us and  russia  usually take the lead in space  exploration while canada  takes a back seat so what can canadians  do to put their mark on the cosmos  mike drewlay looks at the path to space  for the longest time shamira andres  thought her future was on the stage  dancing was her singular passion until  her high school class went to canada's  wonderland  to take measurements on a roller coaster  she eventually had a choice  between the top schools for dance or  science  today she's working as a young graduate  trainee at the european space agency  so you can deduce the patch she chose  but she now sees a connection between  her skills in ballet and science for me  i would really love to be a dancing  astronaut  if that is a profession that i can make  but for now i'm trying to find my  passion in planetary geophysics and  science communication and education  canadians who dream of space are limited  with what they can accomplish here at  home  the canadian space agency chooses our  future astronauts  but to take part in a mission to mars or  fly to the moon  the only option is to go elsewhere  montreal's farah alibay was interested  in the mars rover program  so she got a job with nasa in california  today she's part of a large team behind  the first attempt  to fly a helicopter on another planet  [Music]  canada does have a robust aerospace  industry but the majority of its focus  is on designing and manufacturing parts  for planes and helicopters  here on earth recalibrate the esc where  canada does excel is in education  mcgill professor inna scharf's research  into autonomous vehicles  and space debris attracts international  students who will likely leave canada  after they graduate i hope that they'll  come back eventually  i hope they'll start companies or come  and work for  for the companies that we have here  andres is currently working in the  netherlands and plans to go to france  for her phd  after that who knows she likes to say  there's no linear path to space  no matter where she goes she's certain  of one thing there will be canadians  along the way  oh my gosh i i think we build every each  other up together  honestly and even though we might not  know each other personally that  community in space is very very  inclusive  i feel that i found my people mike delay  global news toronto  you can watch and read more about this  story at globalnews.ca  the new reality and that is global  national for this sunday nigh